#!/bin/sh
# THE FACTORY'S OWN pre-commit gate. Not to be confused with the hook it SHIPS, which lives at
# modules/base/source/.githooks/pre-commit and is payload — edit that one to change generated projects.
#
# Wired via core.hooksPath by the `prepare` script on install. For a deliberate work-in-progress commit,
# bypass with `git commit --no-verify`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. Until the Biome switch the factory had no hook layer: it prescribed a
# pre-commit gate to every project it generated and ran none itself. `core.hooksPath` was unset.
#
# WHY IT RUNS TWO STEPS WHERE THE SHIPPED HOOK RUNS ONE. In a generated project `check:all` covers
# everything that project has, so one command is the whole cheap gate. The factory has a second cheap
# check outside `check:all` — `examples:check`, which proves the committed examples still match what the
# generator produces. That is the single most frequent mistake in this repository's history: change a
# module, forget to refresh the examples. Together they cost about 3.3 seconds, measured.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE, so a passing hook is not read as more than it is:
#   test:prompts, test:cli,   — cheap, but they only fire on generator or CLI changes, and each rebuilds.
#   test:layout
#   verify                    — installs and gates ten generated projects. Minutes. CI's job.
# All four run in .github/workflows/ci.yml on every push and pull request.
#
# No package-manager detection, unlike the shipped hook. That one is copied verbatim into projects using
# any of three managers and must work in all of them; the factory is always npm and commits
# package-lock.json.
set -e

npm run check:all
npm run examples:check
